L000332	
L000333	\noindent{\bf Hagedorn and beyond.}
L000334	We now turn to the Hagedorn singularity of the deformed partition function.
L000335	
L000336	\vspace{0.3cm}
L000337	\noindent{\it - Asymptotic behavior.} To better understand the nature of the singularity, consider the behavior of $\mathcal{T}_{\chi}[Z_E]$ near the cusp $\tau_2\gg 1$. Using the asymptotics of the Eisenstein series, we obtain
L000338	\begin{align}
L000339	\mathcal{T}_{\chi}[Z_E]=&\,\frac{\bar{c}}{2}(U(1,1,4\chi)-\log\tau_2)\\\nonumber
L000340	&\,+\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}C_k(\chi)\frac{1}{k!}\left(\frac{2\pi \tc\tau_2}{12}\right)^k+\cdots
L000341	\end{align}
L000342	where the ellipsis denotes subleading terms in $\tau_2$. Focusing on the infinite sum
L000343	\begin{align}
L000344	&\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}C_k(\chi)\frac{1}{k!}\left(\frac{2\pi \tc\tau_2}{12}\right)^k\\\nonumber
L000345	&=\frac{2}{\sqrt{\pi}}e^{2\chi}\sqrt{\chi}\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{1}{k!}\left(\frac{2\pi \tc\lambda}{12}\chi\right)^kK_{k-\frac{1}{2}}(2\chi)\,,
L000346	\end{align}
L000347	the right-hand side can be evaluated using the multiplication theorem for Bessel functions (see \emph{e.g.} \cite{gradshteyn2014table})
L000348	\begin{align}
L000349	\xi^{-\nu}K_{\nu}(\xi z)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{1}{k!}\left(\frac{(1-\xi^2)z}{2}\right)^k K_{\nu+k}(z)\,,
L000350	\end{align}
L000351	which converges for $|1-\xi^2|<1$. Setting $1-\xi^2=2\pi \tc\lambda/12$ and $z=2\chi$, we obtain
L000352	\begin{align}
L000353	\label{eq:productForC}
L000354	\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}C_k(\chi)\frac{1}{k!}\left(\frac{2\pi \tc\tau_2}{12}\right)^k=\exp\left(2\chi(1-\xi)\right)
L000355	\end{align}
L000356	within the convergence region $|\pi \tc\lambda/6|<1$. For $|\pi \tc \lambda/6|\ge 1$, the series \eqref{eq:productForC} ceases to converge at the boundary, for positive real $\lambda$ this gives the Hagedorn divergence. The right-hand side remains well-defined even for $\lambda$ outside the convergence region, and we can take it as analytic continuation of the left-hand side.
L000357	
L000358	
L000359	
L000360	
L000361	
L000362	\vspace{0.3cm}
L000363	\noindent{\it - Analytic continuation.}  The above analysis provides a natural avenue for analytically continuing the partition function beyond the Hagedorn singularity. Since $\mathcal{T}_{\chi}[Z_R]$ is regular, we focus on $\mathcal{T}_{\chi}[Z_E]$. Using the fact that the Eisenstein series can be expressed as a Poincar\'e series,
L000364	\begin{align}
L000365	E_s(\tau)=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{\gamma\in \Gamma/\Gamma_{\infty}}(\text{Im}\,\gamma\cdot\tau)^s=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{\text{GCD}(c,d)=1}\frac{\tau_2^s}{|c\tau+d|^{2s}}
L000366	\end{align}
L000367	where $\Gamma_{\infty}$ is generated by $\left(\begin{array}{ll}1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1\end{array}\right)$,
L000368	we can write
L000369	\begin{align}
L000370	\mathcal{T}_{\chi}[Z_E]=&\,\sum_{k=2}^{\infty}\frac{1}{k!}\left(\frac{2\pi \tc}{12}\right)^kC_k(\chi) E_k(\tau)+\cdots\\\nonumber
L000371	=&\,\frac{1}{2}\sum_{k=2}^{\infty}\frac{1}{k!}\left(\frac{2\pi \tc}{12}\right)^kC_k(\chi) \sum_{\gamma\in\Gamma/\Gamma_{\infty}}(\text{Im}\,\gamma\cdot\tau)^k+\cdots .
L000372	\end{align}
L000373	Now we exchange the order of the two summations. If $\mathcal{T}_{\chi}[Z_E]$ is absolutely convergent, this interchange is justified; otherwise, we adopt it as \emph{a prescription for analytic continuation}, yielding
L000374	\begin{align}
L000375	\widetilde{\mathcal{T}_{\chi}}[Z_E]&=\,1-\frac{2\pi \tc}{12}\kappa+\frac{2\pi \tc}{12}\left(E_1^r(\tau)+\frac{3}{\pi}U(1,1,4\chi) \right)\nonumber\\
L000376	&\,+\frac{1}{2}\sum_{\gamma\in\Gamma/\Gamma_{\infty}}\left[\exp\left(2\chi\left(1-\sqrt{1-\frac{2\pi \tc}{12\chi}\text{Im}(\gamma\cdot\tau)} \right)\right)\right.\nonumber\\
L000377	&\qquad\qquad\left. -1-\frac{2\pi \tc\,\text{Im}(\gamma\cdot\tau)}{12}\right]
L000378	\label{eq:TZE}
L000379	\end{align}
L000380	where we have used \eqref{eq:productForC}. The Poincar\'e sum in the second line converges, whose proof can be found in the SM, and we take $\widetilde{\mathcal{T}_{\chi}} [Z_E]$ as the analytic continuation of ${\mathcal{T}_{\chi}}[Z_E]$. Consequently, the analytically continued partition function is given by
L000381	\begin{align}
L000382	\widetilde{\mathcal{Z}}(\tau|\lambda)=\widetilde{\mathcal{T}_{\chi}}[Z_E]+\mathcal{T}_{\chi}[Z_R]\,.
L000383	\end{align}
L000384	This constitutes another main result of the present work. We have computed the Poincar\'e sum numerically and confirmed that it indeed converges rapidly.
L000385	
L000386	\vspace{0.3cm}
L000387	\noindent{\it - Hagedorn singularities.}
L000388	After analytic continuation, the partition function is no longer divergent. The Hagedorn singularities are now manifest as branch points of $\widetilde{\mathcal{T}_{\chi}}[Z_E]$. It has a branch point whenever
L000389	\begin{equation}\label{eq:lmb-br}
L000390	    \lambda = \lambda_\gamma \equiv \frac{6}{\pi \tc}\frac{{\rm Im}\tau}{{\rm Im}(\gamma\cdot\tau)},\quad \gamma \in \Gamma/\Gamma_\infty,
L000391	\end{equation}
L000392	and there are infinitely many of them. They are all located on the positive real axis in the complex $\lambda$-plane, and are bounded from below in the range $[\lambda_m,\infty)$, where the minimal branch point $\lambda_m$ is reached when $\gamma$ in \eqref{eq:lmb-br} is such that $\gamma\cdot \tau$ is in the fundamental domain so that the denominator is maximum \footnote{Note that even though there are infinitely many branch points in the range  $[\lambda_m,\infty)$, they are not dense. The location of branch points $\lambda_\gamma$ can be written as
L000393	$\lambda_\gamma = 6/(\pi\tc)((c\tau_1+d)^2+c^2\tau_2^2)$ where $c,d$ are coprime integers, and it is then easy to show that
L000394	there are finitely many $\lambda_\gamma$ small than $T$ for any $T > 0$.}. This is illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:FF-Ze-denplot} for the Ising model. The form of $Z_E$ and $\widetilde{\mathcal{T}_{\chi}}[Z_E]$ are universal and the only difference between different CFTs is the value of $\bar{c}$. Therefore the analytic structure for a generic CFT is very similar to the one shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:FF-Ze-denplot}.
L000395	
